DATA STRATEGY
- I understand that `inst/` is always bundled during deploy because golem apps are installed as R packages. (Explanation: First, by relying on inst/ you are explicitly accepting the R package contract: anything placed there becomes part of the installed artifact and is guaranteed to exist at runtime in every environment (local dev, Shiny Server, Docker, CI). This eliminates an entire class of bugs related to missing files, relative paths, working directories, or environment-specific layouts. Your app no longer depends on “where it’s run from,” only on what’s installed.)
- I access bundled data via `system.file("extdata", package = golem::get_golem_name())` (Explanation: Second, using system.file("extdata", package = golem::get_golem_name()) enforces location transparency. You never assume a filesystem path; instead, you ask R’s package system where the data lives. This makes your code robust to installation paths, versioned libraries, and containerized deployments, and it clearly documents that the data is owned by the package, not by the runtime environment.)
- I do NOT write to this data at runtime. (Explanation: Third, by not writing to this data at runtime, you preserve immutability, which is critical for reliable Shiny apps. Bundled data becomes a read-only reference (lookups, defaults, schemas, demo datasets), ensuring that sessions cannot corrupt shared state, that concurrent users are safe, and that behavior is deterministic across restarts. Any stateful or environment-specific data is correctly pushed outside the package (e.g., env vars, external stores, caches).)
- I may later support external data via environment variables (e.g. DATA_ROOT),
  with snapshot data as a fallback.

